1
00:00:00,988 --> 00:00:04,050
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I've got a sutta here.

2
00:00:06,491 --> 00:00:08,632
[SPEAKER_00]: I've translated it a bit.

3
00:00:08,772 --> 00:00:17,057
[SPEAKER_00]: It's quite different from the other, the sort of standard translations out there.

4
00:00:19,078 --> 00:00:21,139
[SPEAKER_00]: It might raise some interesting points.

5
00:00:21,699 --> 00:00:27,322
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on one occasion, the Buddha was living with the Sambans.

6
00:00:28,263 --> 00:00:30,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Venerable Udayi approaches the Buddha.

7
00:00:34,108 --> 00:00:37,510
[SPEAKER_00]: It is marvelous, Bhante.

8
00:00:37,710 --> 00:00:38,870
[SPEAKER_00]: It is wonderful, Bhante.

9
00:00:39,691 --> 00:00:48,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhante, how much has been done by me and the extent of my respect and my shame and fear of wrongdoing.

10
00:00:48,816 --> 00:00:50,657
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a different translation.

11
00:00:50,677 --> 00:00:54,159
[SPEAKER_00]: I think the standard one is like something like

12
00:00:57,476 --> 00:01:03,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, I think, literally, it's...

13
00:01:03,538 --> 00:01:05,119
[SPEAKER_00]: He's saying how much has been done by me.

14
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:08,901
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah.

15
00:01:09,001 --> 00:01:13,222
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he's sort of commenting on how far he's come.

16
00:01:16,184 --> 00:01:19,525
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, I wasn't doing much with the dhamma.

17
00:01:19,665 --> 00:01:21,206
[SPEAKER_00]: I wasn't doing much with the saṅgha.

18
00:01:21,246 --> 00:01:24,287
[SPEAKER_00]: I wasn't, like, particularly...

19
00:01:27,082 --> 00:01:27,302
[SPEAKER_00]: What?

20
00:01:27,822 --> 00:01:38,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Engage with the dhamma, engage with the saṅgha, but seeing my love for the Buddha, and respect, and shame, and fear of wrongdoing, I went forth from home to homelessness.

21
00:01:39,048 --> 00:01:39,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Hmm.

22
00:01:39,608 --> 00:01:40,789
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just mainly inspired.

23
00:01:41,269 --> 00:01:41,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

24
00:01:42,410 --> 00:01:49,554
[SPEAKER_00]: I think pema, inspiration, devotion, it's that kind of...

25
00:01:49,574 --> 00:01:53,075
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's often, like, a negative thing.

26
00:01:53,175 --> 00:01:53,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

27
00:01:53,916 --> 00:01:54,456
[SPEAKER_00]: Love, attachment.

28
00:01:54,476 --> 00:01:55,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Completely based on ignorance.

29
00:01:55,657 --> 00:01:55,857
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

30
00:01:56,608 --> 00:01:58,409
[SPEAKER_01]: And, uh, infatuation almost.

31
00:01:58,549 --> 00:01:59,389
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

32
00:02:00,069 --> 00:02:06,591
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, it can have this positive, um, effect.

33
00:02:06,611 --> 00:02:07,011
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah.

34
00:02:07,031 --> 00:02:13,213
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seeing my love for the auspicious one and respect for shame and fear of wrongdoing, I went forth from hometownlessness.

35
00:02:14,253 --> 00:02:21,655
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha taught me that dhamma, such as matter, such as the arising of matter, such as the disappearing of matter, such as feeling,

36
00:02:22,209 --> 00:02:28,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Bante, having gone forth to an empty dwelling, pondering over, turning round, the upside-downness of the five assumed aggregates,

37
00:02:44,251 --> 00:02:47,133
[SPEAKER_00]: I discerned the essence of this is suffering.

38
00:02:47,933 --> 00:02:50,874
[SPEAKER_00]: I discerned the essence of this is the arising of suffering.

39
00:02:51,955 --> 00:02:54,877
[SPEAKER_00]: I discerned the essence of this is the cessation of suffering.

40
00:02:55,817 --> 00:02:59,679
[SPEAKER_00]: And I discerned the essence of this is the way leading to the cessation of suffering.

41
00:02:59,699 --> 00:03:03,601
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by discerning the right order, he discerned the Four Noble Truths.

42
00:03:05,682 --> 00:03:12,486
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might, maybe, if you say about the Pali of that, because you might sound as if... because we keep talking about the right order.

43
00:03:12,586 --> 00:03:12,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

44
00:03:13,529 --> 00:03:19,612
[SPEAKER_00]: It might sound as if the translation tries to read into the sutta what we wanted to say.

45
00:03:20,012 --> 00:03:20,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, I can imagine.

46
00:03:20,852 --> 00:03:23,793
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you look at the Pāli then, what does the Pāli say?

47
00:03:24,113 --> 00:03:27,935
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the normal translation for this, I should say...

48
00:03:27,955 --> 00:03:34,858
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Pāli is ukuja and avakuja together as a compound.

49
00:03:35,698 --> 00:03:39,860
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the commentary, it takes that to mean

50
00:03:41,615 --> 00:03:51,319
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ukuja as rise, and avakuja as fall, and it says something like it's... hang on, what's this how it says it here?

51
00:03:51,599 --> 00:03:55,941
[SPEAKER_00]: The arising is called surge, akuja, and the fall is decline.

52
00:03:56,262 --> 00:04:00,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, he was exploring the aggregates by way of rise and fall.

53
00:04:00,683 --> 00:04:01,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Udayabhaya.

54
00:04:03,405 --> 00:04:04,625
[SPEAKER_01]: See, there's only the problem with that.

55
00:04:04,685 --> 00:04:06,046
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you explore

56
00:04:07,428 --> 00:04:18,492
[SPEAKER_01]: What you are, what the basis of your sense of self is, how can you see that decline, surge, unless you assume yourself separate from it, so you can look at it?

57
00:04:19,452 --> 00:04:27,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, when your own I is subjected to rise and fall, if you want to take that to mean that, how can you see that?

58
00:04:28,988 --> 00:04:30,910
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, I can only see the sights.

59
00:04:31,410 --> 00:04:33,532
[SPEAKER_01]: How can I have a sight of itself?

60
00:04:33,932 --> 00:04:34,633
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

61
00:04:35,193 --> 00:04:39,857
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can then I understand the nature of itself if it merely knows how to approach it through sights?

62
00:04:40,578 --> 00:04:40,938
[SPEAKER_00]: It can't.

63
00:04:41,459 --> 00:04:44,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Nature is unattainable.

64
00:04:46,503 --> 00:04:49,906
[SPEAKER_01]: An understanding of it is unattainable if all your eye can do is see.

65
00:04:51,227 --> 00:04:53,088
[SPEAKER_01]: If there's no knowledge in regard to the eye.

66
00:04:55,612 --> 00:05:04,097
[SPEAKER_01]: And that whole description of the surge, the fall, always ties in with the view of how you just have to keep observing things changing, keep observing things changing.

67
00:05:04,137 --> 00:05:05,418
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still an observation.

68
00:05:05,678 --> 00:05:07,659
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, the observer remains completely unknown.

69
00:05:08,459 --> 00:05:11,301
[SPEAKER_00]: But there are places in the suttas where people...

70
00:05:12,782 --> 00:05:16,544
[SPEAKER_01]: They are, but that's on the level of endurance, what we discussed before.

71
00:05:17,408 --> 00:05:19,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Even that is not on the level of observation.

72
00:05:19,610 --> 00:05:19,950
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

73
00:05:19,970 --> 00:05:20,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing.

74
00:05:21,091 --> 00:05:21,312
[SPEAKER_00]: It is.

75
00:05:21,512 --> 00:05:21,972
[SPEAKER_00]: It's seeing.

76
00:05:22,052 --> 00:05:22,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing.

77
00:05:22,573 --> 00:05:23,394
[SPEAKER_00]: So... so...

78
00:05:23,714 --> 00:05:29,039
[SPEAKER_00]: I've got... so... Bodhisattva Vipassi.

79
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:30,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

80
00:05:30,980 --> 00:05:35,665
[SPEAKER_00]: He dwell... dwells as one who sees rising his... What?

81
00:05:35,685 --> 00:05:36,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Sees with his eyes?

82
00:05:36,906 --> 00:05:37,366
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

83
00:05:37,587 --> 00:05:37,787
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

84
00:05:37,907 --> 00:05:38,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Sees, regards...

85
00:05:38,768 --> 00:05:40,009
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's not what I'm talking about.

86
00:05:40,029 --> 00:05:40,389
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

87
00:05:40,409 --> 00:05:41,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

88
00:05:41,270 --> 00:05:42,811
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's no reason to bring it up.

89
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:48,182
[SPEAKER_00]: But, so, but there, rising and falling in the five assumed aggregates.

90
00:05:48,262 --> 00:05:52,704
[SPEAKER_00]: This is matter, this is the origin, this is the arising, this is the disappearing.

91
00:05:54,525 --> 00:06:01,889
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you see matter, you see... it's... Again, again, let's not conflate the two.

92
00:06:02,909 --> 00:06:03,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

93
00:06:03,229 --> 00:06:05,170
[SPEAKER_01]: What I was saying is seeing with the eyes.

94
00:06:05,630 --> 00:06:09,072
[SPEAKER_01]: The view is always where you just have to observe the change in the aggregates.

95
00:06:09,592 --> 00:06:12,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you're using word seeing in a sense like, oh, I saw the truth.

96
00:06:12,978 --> 00:06:13,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

97
00:06:13,638 --> 00:06:16,761
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm referring to the actual seeing, literal seeing with your eyes.

98
00:06:17,281 --> 00:06:18,342
[SPEAKER_01]: So, none of that applies.

99
00:06:18,982 --> 00:06:20,523
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha Vipassana was exactly that.

100
00:06:20,863 --> 00:06:25,247
[SPEAKER_01]: He understood the nature of the five aggregates before he became the Buddha.

101
00:06:25,507 --> 00:06:25,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

102
00:06:26,087 --> 00:06:27,368
[SPEAKER_01]: And then observed that.

103
00:06:27,468 --> 00:06:28,128
[SPEAKER_01]: But with right view.

104
00:06:28,329 --> 00:06:29,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course, of course.

105
00:06:29,309 --> 00:06:30,490
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the right view is.

106
00:06:31,251 --> 00:06:33,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he observed that, or he was seeing it.

107
00:06:34,093 --> 00:06:34,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

108
00:06:35,123 --> 00:06:38,585
[SPEAKER_01]: And, simply by dwelling on that, he became a fully enlightened Buddha.

109
00:06:38,665 --> 00:06:38,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

110
00:06:39,125 --> 00:06:42,426
[SPEAKER_01]: But not by actually seeing it with his eyes and looking at it.

111
00:06:42,506 --> 00:06:43,267
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he sees...

112
00:06:43,287 --> 00:06:44,267
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's metaphorically.

113
00:06:44,387 --> 00:06:44,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry.

114
00:06:44,707 --> 00:06:45,448
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah.

115
00:06:45,468 --> 00:06:46,688
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he understands, let's say.

116
00:06:46,808 --> 00:06:47,428
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands.

117
00:06:47,448 --> 00:06:48,929
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

118
00:06:49,829 --> 00:06:55,792
[SPEAKER_00]: He dwells, he remains, he sort of spends time understanding... Hmm.

119
00:06:56,512 --> 00:06:56,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

120
00:06:56,712 --> 00:07:02,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Not only has... have the aggregates... each aggregate has manifested, but

121
00:07:03,973 --> 00:07:05,975
[SPEAKER_00]: This is... there's a rising.

122
00:07:06,315 --> 00:07:11,839
[SPEAKER_00]: This is like a increasing in its presence, or it's decreasing in its presence.

123
00:07:11,879 --> 00:07:13,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, it's manifested.

124
00:07:13,961 --> 00:07:14,161
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

125
00:07:14,622 --> 00:07:17,544
[SPEAKER_00]: So, such is matter, such is the arising of matter, such is the...

126
00:07:17,604 --> 00:07:28,953
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, say, in regards to sights, something you see, something that maybe it's affecting you either with passion or aversion, say, a displeasing sight... Yeah.

