192
00:10:50,167 --> 00:10:50,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

193
00:10:50,587 --> 00:10:50,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

194
00:10:50,788 --> 00:10:52,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, no, it's an accurate way to express.

195
00:10:52,188 --> 00:10:54,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would you express it inaccurately, even if you're related?

196
00:10:55,608 --> 00:10:56,969
[SPEAKER_01]: On account of accurate understanding.

197
00:10:57,389 --> 00:10:57,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

198
00:10:57,749 --> 00:10:58,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the whole point.

199
00:10:58,569 --> 00:10:58,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

200
00:10:58,829 --> 00:11:00,089
[SPEAKER_01]: You accurately understood now.

201
00:11:00,449 --> 00:11:00,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

202
00:11:00,770 --> 00:11:03,190
[SPEAKER_00]: But now you're gonna inaccurately describe it.

203
00:11:03,790 --> 00:11:04,450
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't make sense.

204
00:11:04,470 --> 00:11:09,951
[SPEAKER_01]: But anyway, this is even more pertaining to... No, he's actually contemplating the right order of things.

205
00:11:10,011 --> 00:11:10,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

206
00:11:11,272 --> 00:11:13,092
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the wrong order?

207
00:11:15,729 --> 00:11:19,772
[SPEAKER_01]: From the point of view of your personal experience, what would constitute the wrong order?

208
00:11:20,352 --> 00:11:22,733
[SPEAKER_01]: I come first, and these aggregates are related to me.

209
00:11:23,194 --> 00:11:25,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Me and everything else in the relationship.

210
00:11:26,076 --> 00:11:26,716
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they're mine.

211
00:11:26,796 --> 00:11:29,217
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this matter is mine, or it's in me, or it's...

212
00:11:29,398 --> 00:11:30,678
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm in the matter, or whatever.

213
00:11:30,698 --> 00:11:32,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you come first.

214
00:11:32,359 --> 00:11:32,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

215
00:11:32,700 --> 00:11:34,701
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the point, that self-centredness of it.

216
00:11:35,561 --> 00:11:39,744
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you understand, it doesn't matter how self-centred your sense of self might feel, how in control, how much...

217
00:11:42,071 --> 00:11:46,374
[SPEAKER_01]: The more present it is, it's present within the five aggregates.

218
00:11:46,474 --> 00:11:47,275
[SPEAKER_01]: It's second to them.

219
00:11:47,435 --> 00:11:48,436
[SPEAKER_01]: It's dependent on them.

220
00:11:48,856 --> 00:11:49,837
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not on the way around.

221
00:11:51,418 --> 00:11:53,540
[SPEAKER_01]: So, literally, it's the same thing that remains there.

222
00:11:53,960 --> 00:11:57,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Just, instead of placing it like this, you place it like this.

223
00:11:58,463 --> 00:12:00,925
[SPEAKER_01]: The emphasis is somewhere else and everything changes.

224
00:12:00,945 --> 00:12:01,145
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

225
00:12:01,165 --> 00:12:02,526
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't create new things.

226
00:12:02,686 --> 00:12:06,729
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't introduce new components into the five aggregates when you start practicing the dhamma.

227
00:12:06,749 --> 00:12:07,290
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

228
00:12:08,143 --> 00:12:12,564
[SPEAKER_00]: But the whole emotional significance of the picture is now completely different.

229
00:12:12,784 --> 00:12:13,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, of course.

230
00:12:13,484 --> 00:12:16,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, everything... because the whole saṃsāra depends on the wrong order.

231
00:12:18,406 --> 00:12:19,366
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not... that's what I mean.

232
00:12:19,386 --> 00:12:24,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, saṃsāra is not outside of something... a universe of rebirths where you just travel through.

233
00:12:24,287 --> 00:12:25,407
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's within your point of view.

234
00:12:25,607 --> 00:12:26,668
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what your point of view is.

235
00:12:27,688 --> 00:12:28,748
[SPEAKER_01]: Saṃsāra is the wrong view.

236
00:12:28,768 --> 00:12:30,589
[SPEAKER_01]: Saṃsāra is the structure of the wrong order.

237
00:12:31,429 --> 00:12:33,309
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for as long as that's there, you'll keep reappearing.

238
00:12:34,650 --> 00:12:35,510
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll keep enduring.

239
00:12:36,897 --> 00:12:43,339
[SPEAKER_00]: But by contemplating, turning that round, one can discern the Four Noble Truths.

240
00:12:44,580 --> 00:12:45,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, yeah.

241
00:12:45,280 --> 00:12:51,842
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, that's how it was expressed there, where you can pretty much discern anything that the Buddha was teaching about, because it's the principle of Dītsuṃpāda.

242
00:12:52,182 --> 00:12:52,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

243
00:12:52,843 --> 00:12:53,743
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is.

244
00:12:53,903 --> 00:12:57,904
[SPEAKER_01]: With this first, this is second, as in description of the wrong order.

245
00:12:58,164 --> 00:12:58,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

246
00:12:58,664 --> 00:12:59,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, without this as first,

247
00:13:00,655 --> 00:13:01,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Have you had a second?

248
00:13:01,535 --> 00:13:02,716
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a description of the right order.

249
00:13:03,136 --> 00:13:04,757
[SPEAKER_01]: So, with the grain, against the grain.

250
00:13:05,177 --> 00:13:07,278
[SPEAKER_00]: All those things that the Buddha used to describe patitsaṁpada.

251
00:13:09,859 --> 00:13:10,340
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what it is.

252
00:13:10,360 --> 00:13:13,801
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's... and again, even in Putujana's experience, there is patitsaṁpada.

253
00:13:14,481 --> 00:13:21,565
[SPEAKER_01]: There is sense of I am, and everything else that belongs to me, that I can use, act, see, hear, taste, touch, think about, and so on.

254
00:13:23,025 --> 00:13:24,646
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the patitsaṁpada with that grain.

255
00:13:25,046 --> 00:13:26,587
[SPEAKER_01]: You go with the grain of ignorance.

256
00:13:26,947 --> 00:13:28,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Knee towards the world.

257
00:13:29,295 --> 00:13:31,296
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's still me and the world, if you want to call it like that.

258
00:13:31,656 --> 00:13:36,697
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you realize the world, as in this body, the material world, the senses, it's the container.

259
00:13:36,737 --> 00:13:40,198
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a necessary basis for the existence of my notion of self.

260
00:13:41,039 --> 00:13:42,459
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm not denying my notion of self.

261
00:13:42,519 --> 00:13:49,041
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm just not seeing it as mine anymore, because it doesn't depend on me wanting to keep it, but it depends on these things allowing it to be.

262
00:13:51,182 --> 00:13:51,942
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's still the same thing.

263
00:13:52,162 --> 00:13:53,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Me and the matter.

264
00:13:53,342 --> 00:13:54,023
[SPEAKER_01]: Me and the world.

265
00:13:56,666 --> 00:13:59,348
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, its emphasis is, oh, this is the first, actually.

266
00:13:59,969 --> 00:14:00,689
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the second.

267
00:14:00,769 --> 00:14:02,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, this is the first and that's the second.

268
00:14:05,853 --> 00:14:07,375
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what it's all about.

269
00:14:07,815 --> 00:14:10,077
[SPEAKER_01]: In its principle, it's described as, with this, this is.

270
00:14:10,837 --> 00:14:11,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

271
00:14:11,598 --> 00:14:12,559
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is.

272
00:14:13,299 --> 00:14:13,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Two things.

273
00:14:13,580 --> 00:14:14,080
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the dhamma.

274
00:14:14,220 --> 00:14:14,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

275
00:14:15,061 --> 00:14:15,942
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is.

276
00:14:16,582 --> 00:14:16,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

277
00:14:18,303 --> 00:14:19,404
[SPEAKER_01]: Without this, this won't be.

278
00:14:19,825 --> 00:14:20,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

279
00:14:20,145 --> 00:14:22,127
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if this is not first, this cannot be the second.

280
00:14:22,287 --> 00:14:22,507
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

281
00:14:30,375 --> 00:14:36,437
[SPEAKER_01]: And contemplating the right order, it's probably something a patujanā can do.

282
00:14:36,997 --> 00:14:39,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's what I think what's going on here.

283
00:14:39,698 --> 00:14:45,119
[SPEAKER_00]: He's describing himself as a patujanā, pondering over, turning around.

284
00:14:45,159 --> 00:14:48,220
[SPEAKER_00]: It's almost as if, what if I were to turn around?

285
00:14:48,600 --> 00:14:55,222
[SPEAKER_00]: What if I were to, out of mere faith in the Buddha and his teaching, out of the love for him, regard this as first and this as second?

286
00:14:55,982 --> 00:14:57,423
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't seem natural to me.

287
00:14:58,392 --> 00:15:00,833
[SPEAKER_01]: He contradicts... he goes against the grain of my experience.

288
00:15:00,853 --> 00:15:02,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, there's nothing that I've got...

289
00:15:02,273 --> 00:15:05,054
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no validation immediate that it's true, that it's right.

290
00:15:05,094 --> 00:15:06,655
[SPEAKER_01]: But what if I were to do it out of mere faith?

291
00:15:06,855 --> 00:15:10,276
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly the faith type in the suttas they describe.

292
00:15:11,077 --> 00:15:16,298
[SPEAKER_01]: People think faith type means you're just purely devoted, infatuated, and somehow you become an arahant on account of infatuation.

293
00:15:16,319 --> 00:15:16,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

294
00:15:16,779 --> 00:15:16,859
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

295
00:15:16,879 --> 00:15:19,240
[SPEAKER_01]: It means that you start applying the dhamma on account of faith.

296
00:15:19,860 --> 00:15:20,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

297
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:23,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, somebody else would need to make some sense.

298
00:15:23,701 --> 00:15:25,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would the right order work?

299
00:15:25,662 --> 00:15:25,842
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

300
00:15:26,782 --> 00:15:34,044
[SPEAKER_01]: And based on getting some intellectual understanding why that order would work, he gets to apply the right order, and because it is a right order, it will work.

301
00:15:34,824 --> 00:15:39,505
[SPEAKER_01]: And the result of it, the verification of it working, is being free from suffering amidst of suffering.

302
00:15:39,905 --> 00:15:40,505
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't suffer.

303
00:15:41,105 --> 00:15:42,365
[SPEAKER_01]: You're incapable of suffering.

304
00:15:44,126 --> 00:15:46,186
[SPEAKER_01]: And, oh yeah, it worked.

305
00:15:46,986 --> 00:15:55,428
[SPEAKER_01]: But somebody who doesn't necessarily ponder on it, can't make sense of it, could just do it because they understood to the extent that that's what the Buddha points at.

306
00:15:56,526 --> 00:16:02,689
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why even the faith type, even him over there, because he was clearly a faith type, as he said himself.

307
00:16:02,769 --> 00:16:04,410
[SPEAKER_01]: He was just inspired.

308
00:16:04,430 --> 00:16:06,831
[SPEAKER_01]: He had no relation to the Saṅgha or nothing, basically.

309
00:16:06,851 --> 00:16:10,713
[SPEAKER_01]: He was just inspired by the Buddha and that's why he became a monk.

310
00:16:12,214 --> 00:16:23,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Even he was then, you know, the fact that he was able to at least get the notion of, oh, maybe I should put this first and this second, means that he had some grasp, some instruction that he made sense of.

311
00:16:24,758 --> 00:16:28,039
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the difference between asuttava-putujjana and suttava-putujjana.

312
00:16:28,079 --> 00:16:28,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

313
00:16:29,499 --> 00:16:29,899
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't think...

314
00:16:29,959 --> 00:16:30,299
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't think...

315
00:16:30,479 --> 00:16:34,060
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't think it actually says...

316
00:16:35,300 --> 00:16:41,021
[SPEAKER_00]: There's suttava-bhikkhu in the suttas, but I don't think you ever actually see suttava-putujjana in the suttas.

317
00:16:41,041 --> 00:16:41,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right, right.

318
00:16:41,801 --> 00:16:42,782
[SPEAKER_01]: You just see asuttava-putujjana.

319
00:16:42,802 --> 00:16:44,382
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it's always... yeah, I think it's always the suttava-putujjana.

320
00:16:44,402 --> 00:16:46,282
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you can infer that there is a suttava-putujjana.

321
00:16:46,322 --> 00:16:46,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah

322
00:16:50,161 --> 00:16:53,022
[SPEAKER_00]: So, someone who's had some instruction doesn't see it for themselves.

323
00:16:53,142 --> 00:16:53,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

324
00:16:53,922 --> 00:17:02,104
[SPEAKER_00]: But, see, so now, that puthujjana, who understood the right order, at least got the notion of it... Got the notion, yeah.

325
00:17:02,144 --> 00:17:08,286
[SPEAKER_01]: ...can start to apply it on account of the faith in the Buddha, because he thinks the Buddha is the greatest, and he'll just do whatever the Buddha says.

326
00:17:09,746 --> 00:17:13,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, if he doesn't have such faith, he can then start applying it after pondering on it.

327
00:17:14,487 --> 00:17:14,627
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

328
00:17:14,767 --> 00:17:17,628
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, he thinks about it, and he's like, what actually makes more sense?

329
00:17:17,848 --> 00:17:18,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

330
00:17:19,284 --> 00:17:23,666
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I were to try to do this, and sustain this, and cultivate this, and develop this?

331
00:17:25,186 --> 00:17:27,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, that person would arrive at the right view.

332
00:17:28,327 --> 00:17:30,928
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter whether you came through faith or through investigation.

333
00:17:31,188 --> 00:17:32,529
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is to arrive at the right view.

334
00:17:36,590 --> 00:17:44,433
[SPEAKER_01]: So the first... What would be the necessary condition for a patujana to be able to start practicing the right order?

335
00:17:46,020 --> 00:17:53,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Except understanding that there is such thing as right order, what what what he needs to do in order to understand that?

336
00:17:54,407 --> 00:17:55,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, what what?

337
00:17:55,989 --> 00:17:56,910
[SPEAKER_01]: So, Bhutujana... Yeah.

338
00:17:56,950 --> 00:18:03,355
[SPEAKER_00]: ...who has some notion of the teaching... His hands got stuck, eh?

339
00:18:04,436 --> 00:18:04,656
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

340
00:18:04,696 --> 00:18:05,617
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to...

341
00:18:05,858 --> 00:18:13,124
[SPEAKER_01]: In order to start pondering on the right order, as the Buddha did, what does... he must stop doing?

342
00:18:15,050 --> 00:18:15,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, okay.

343
00:18:15,911 --> 00:18:24,276
[SPEAKER_00]: He needs to, first of all, practice restraint and stop gratifying his desires, basically.

344
00:18:24,336 --> 00:18:26,257
[SPEAKER_00]: Secondly?

345
00:18:26,337 --> 00:18:32,400
[SPEAKER_00]: So, keep precepts, practice restraint and spend time on his own.

346
00:18:33,081 --> 00:18:34,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's on that level of a lifestyle.

347
00:18:35,362 --> 00:18:35,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

348
00:18:36,123 --> 00:18:37,223
[SPEAKER_01]: But it would be the second thing.

349
00:18:37,463 --> 00:18:38,444
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of more internal.

350
00:18:38,784 --> 00:18:40,005
[SPEAKER_01]: What would he have to stop doing?

351
00:18:44,038 --> 00:18:48,201
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as we described, the order is the sense of self and the... and the... and everything else.

352
00:18:49,022 --> 00:18:49,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

353
00:18:50,003 --> 00:18:52,205
[SPEAKER_01]: But the right order is everything else and the sense of self.

354
00:18:52,225 --> 00:18:52,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

355
00:18:52,965 --> 00:18:55,887
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he needs to start including everything else.

356
00:18:56,428 --> 00:19:03,233
[SPEAKER_01]: He needs to start including, but above all, he needs to stop either ignoring the sense of self or trying to get rid of it.

357
00:19:03,273 --> 00:19:03,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

358
00:19:03,794 --> 00:19:04,034
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

359
00:19:04,074 --> 00:19:06,456
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's, practically speaking, what most people who

360
00:19:07,016 --> 00:19:12,218
[SPEAKER_01]: God, the notion of the Buddha's teaching, would be doing, ah, there is no self, there is no self.

361
00:19:12,278 --> 00:19:15,179
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, just, everything serves to, like, overriding the sense of self.

362
00:19:15,199 --> 00:19:15,999
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not what he's doing.

363
00:19:16,039 --> 00:19:18,560
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not saying, I'm... No, of course, of course.

364
00:19:18,660 --> 00:19:20,301
[SPEAKER_00]: All it is, is just changing the priority.

365
00:19:20,581 --> 00:19:23,762
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you change the priority to things if you keep denying one of those things?

366
00:19:23,782 --> 00:19:24,303
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not...

367
00:19:24,343 --> 00:19:27,684
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not acknowledging it, and you tell, oh, it's not real, the sense is real.

368
00:19:27,944 --> 00:19:28,664
[SPEAKER_01]: It's very real.

369
00:19:28,824 --> 00:19:29,064
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

370
00:19:29,905 --> 00:19:30,745
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not first.

371
00:19:30,905 --> 00:19:31,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

372
00:19:31,225 --> 00:19:31,845
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the second

373
00:19:37,871 --> 00:19:50,059
[SPEAKER_01]: Recognizing the self that has manifested right here, in the way, in the kind of... Well, I am, to whatever, yeah, whatever, whatever ambiguous sense I might be, if I try to overly define it, I do have a sense of self.

374
00:19:50,080 --> 00:19:53,002
[SPEAKER_01]: I am such-and-such, a certain individual, something.

375
00:19:53,022 --> 00:20:00,447
[SPEAKER_01]: There is this, like, a group of characteristics, so to speak, that do kind of outline the phenomenon of I am.

376
00:20:00,647 --> 00:20:00,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

377
00:20:01,267 --> 00:20:01,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever.

378
00:20:01,607 --> 00:20:04,088
[SPEAKER_01]: I might be different or whatever, but there is still sense of I am.

379
00:20:04,948 --> 00:20:12,729
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason why people struggle with it is because they always depend on, like, overly defining it in order for it to be clear or real, but...

380
00:20:12,749 --> 00:20:14,289
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a thing until it's...

381
00:20:14,329 --> 00:20:15,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Exact.

382
00:20:15,050 --> 00:20:15,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Clarified.

383
00:20:15,610 --> 00:20:17,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Until I can put my finger on it.

384
00:20:17,130 --> 00:20:17,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

385
00:20:17,510 --> 00:20:22,491
[SPEAKER_01]: See, the direction of putting a finger on it, even if there's nothing to put a finger on it, it's real as such.

386
00:20:22,511 --> 00:20:23,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

387
00:20:23,971 --> 00:20:28,052
[SPEAKER_01]: So you realize, oh, so you can put a finger on the direction where you will be putting a finger on.

388
00:20:28,212 --> 00:20:28,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

389
00:20:28,712 --> 00:20:32,394
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're unaware of it, because you only want to put your finger on something, only that's real.

390
00:20:32,494 --> 00:20:37,797
[SPEAKER_00]: If you... basically, your attempts to clarify something need that something to be there, first of all.

391
00:20:37,917 --> 00:20:38,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

392
00:20:38,718 --> 00:20:40,018
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's, I mean, the sense of self.

393
00:20:40,259 --> 00:20:40,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

394
00:20:40,419 --> 00:20:43,260
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't make it more specific than that.

395
00:20:43,400 --> 00:20:43,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

396
00:20:44,361 --> 00:20:46,282
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you also must stop denying.

397
00:20:47,450 --> 00:20:49,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there's no sense of self, everything is...

398
00:20:49,112 --> 00:20:51,633
[SPEAKER_01]: The person who I love told me that that doesn't exist.

399
00:20:51,694 --> 00:20:52,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

400
00:20:52,154 --> 00:20:52,854
